 "narrative": "The new high-quality-screen and clean-background culture checks strengthen the three strongest accepted tumor-suppressor associations and give additional, though still more cautious, support for PTEN loss. For SMARCA4 loss, H109 in high-quality screens gives +0.832 [0.750, 0.914] and H100 in a clean-background adherent setting gives +0.836 [0.781, 0.891], both far above the 0.15 threshold in the stronger-dependency direction. For KEAP1 loss, H110 gives +0.807 [0.728, 0.886] and H101 gives +0.827 [0.783, 0.871] in the weaker-dependency direction, again far above threshold. For TP53 loss, H111 gives +0.436 [0.321, 0.550] and H102 gives +0.434 [0.318, 0.550], also above threshold. These are not outcome-dependent restrictions; screen_roc_auc is measured quality, and the clean-background contrasts exclude other accepted losses. Conditioning on co-loss absence changes the estimand toward a more isolated association, but it does not create outcome leakage. These checks also reduce concern that the strong SMARCA4/KEAP1/TP53 associations are driven by low-quality screens, dominant culture context, or co-occurring losses.\n\nPTEN loss remains the weakest accepted predictor. The high-quality broad contrast H112 is modest: +0.169 [0.050, 0.287], with the point estimate above threshold but the lower interval bound below it. The clean-background high-quality PTEN contrast H113 is +0.197 [0.101, 0.293], and the clean-background high-quality adenocarcinoma contrast H114 is +0.190 [0.084, 0.296]. The clean-background adherent PTEN contrast H103 is stronger and more favorable: +0.265 [0.170, 0.360], with the lower bound above threshold. Direct sex-specific PTEN contrasts are similar to each other and to the overall pattern: male +0.234 [0.102, 0.366], female +0.248 [0.100, 0.397]. I accept these as consistent dataset-internal support for a modest PTEN-loss association, especially where multiple independent contexts remain positive, but several intervals do not exclude the threshold or null. I therefore keep PTEN broad/high-quality claims active, defer narrower sex and high-quality adenocarcinoma contexts, and request independent validation of H103 because it is the strongest clean-context PTEN estimate. The H115/H116 results are not sex-interaction claims and should not be interpreted as evidence that PTEN works differently by sex; they are simply subgroup replications. Multiplicity remains a practical concern for PTEN because it is near threshold across many analyses, which motivates the validation request.",
 "research_notes": "Current robust accepted dataset associations: SMARCA4 loss -> lower dependency_composite/stronger dependency, broadly, clean, non-Avana, clean adenocarcinoma, high-quality screen, clean adherent; KEAP1 loss -> higher dependency_composite/weaker dependency, broadly, validated clean, four-background, non-Avana, clean adenocarcinoma, high-quality screen, clean adherent; TP53 loss -> higher dependency_composite/weaker dependency, validated clean four-background, clean adenocarcinoma, high-quality screen, clean adherent. PTEN loss -> modest lower dependency_composite/stronger dependency. Broad R24 +0.227 [0.131,0.323], validated clean R39 +0.241 [0.122,0.360], clean adherent R61 +0.265 [0.170,0.360], high-quality R57 +0.169 [0.050,0.287], clean high-quality R62 +0.197 [0.101,0.293], male/female broad +0.234/+0.248 with lower bounds below threshold. Requested validation: H103 PTEN clean-background adherent. After H103 validation, if independent lower bound remains above or near threshold with consistent direction, treat PTEN as reasonably robust but still modest; if validation falls far below, keep PTEN cautious and avoid strong subgroup claims. Do not overinterpret H115/H116 as sex-specific effects. Close established context checks: H100, H101, H102, H109, H110, H111, H63. Keep PTEN context claims H52/H53/H103/H112/H113 active or as response to validation; defer H114-H116 unless validation or interactions require them. Remaining broad exploration can test interactions or other predictors if budget allows, but priority is PTEN validation and avoiding overacceptance near threshold.",
